Most time during a application production, a team has to work together and develop that application.
So the team will create two environments "Development" and "Production". Each person on the team  
will have their own Development environment and changes to their Development environment will  
have to be checked before adding those changes to the Production environment. Well, that, my friend,  
is how branches work in GitHub.com and Git. We can create a snapshot of or from a main branch repository.  
Then we can work from that created copy of or from that main branch, but all works will be done in the  
new branch not the main branch. Instead of experimenting, testing added features or fixing bugs in a program  
in the main branch and then breaking the code in the main branch; one can create a new branch from the  
traget main branch then perform all experiments and testing in the newly created branch away from the target  
main branch. To compare our story environments with GitHub and Git...    
Development environment is our snapshot/copied newly created branch and Production is our main branch.  

